\begin{document}
	
	\maketitle
	
	\begin{abstract}
		Modern robotics suffers from a fundamental methodological divide between opaque, black-box physics simulators and interpretable, white-box analytical mechanics. While contemporary physics engines (e.g., Simscape Multibody, MuJoCo, Gazebo, PyBullet) effortlessly simulate complex articulated manipulators directly from CAD-derived Unified Robot Description Format (URDF) files, they operate as numerical oracles that conceal the closed-form algebraic structure of the inertia, Coriolis, and gravity matrices indispensable for nonlinear control synthesis, Lyapunov stability proofs, and parameter observability analysis. Conversely, translating arbitrary URDF kinematic graphs into minimal Denavit--Hartenberg (DH) parameterizations and closed-form Euler--Lagrange equations is hindered by topological branching, continuous gauge freedoms in parallel and collinear axes, non-trivial inertial tensor frame projections, and severe symbolic expression explosion. Here, we present a unified, self-verifying URDF-to-Analytical Digital Twin framework realized across three complementary architectural paradigms: (1) Native MATLAB Symbolic Math for maximal glass-box algebraic inspectability and pedagogical verification, (2) MATLAB/CasADi for accelerated industrial Model-Based Design (MBD) with programmatic Simscape Multibody cross-compilation, and (3) Python/CasADi coupled with headless MuJoCo for open-source, real-time closed-loop nonlinear control ($>1$~kHz). We introduce offline derivation-phase symbolic optimizations that eliminate classical symbolic bottlenecks: by replacing rank-3 Christoffel symbol tensors with a direct Euler--Lagrange generalized momentum directional derivative, $\mathbf{c}_{\text{bias}}(\bm{q},\dot{\bm{q}}) = \frac{d}{dt}(\bm{M}(\bm{q})\dot{\bm{q}}) - \frac{1}{2}\nabla_{\bm{q}}(\dot{\bm{q}}^T\bm{M}(\bm{q})\dot{\bm{q}})$, we reduce the asymptotic complexity of Coriolis evaluation from $O(n^4)$ to $O(n^2)$ and slash the forward dynamics symbolic instruction graph by up to $76.8\%$ (e.g., from 307,056 to 75,103 operations at $n=14$) while matching Christoffel evaluations to exact machine precision ($< 10^{-14}$~N$\cdot$m). Coupling this reformulation with an analytical symbolic Cholesky factorization ($\bm{M}(\bm{q}) = \bm{R}(\bm{q})^T\bm{R}(\bm{q})$) accelerates forward dynamics by up to $3.94\times$ over explicit matrix inversion. In addition to a systematic multi-DOF scalability benchmark across serial chains spanning $n \in [3, 14]$ degrees of freedom that confirms an empirical complexity scaling of $\mathcal{O}(n^{2.28})$ versus $\mathcal{O}(n^{3.24})$ for the classical baseline, our framework is validated across four multi-vendor industrial manipulators (Universal Robots UR5 and UR16e, ABB IRB 120, and Unimation Puma 560). The pipeline guarantees machine-precision kinematic and dynamic parity ($<10^{-12}$), evaluates raw inverse dynamics in $149.0~\mu$s ($6.71$~kHz) on the heavy-payload UR16e, and executes 5,000 batch forward integration steps in $0.4035$~s ($12.4\times$ faster than real-time on Earth and $14.5\times$ in lunar gravity). Furthermore, we report an empirical, closed-loop Nonlinear Model Predictive Control (NMPC) benchmark on the heavy-payload UR16e ($n=6$) and kinematically redundant KUKA LBR iiwa 7 ($n=7$) under multi-axis trajectory tracking, actuator torque saturation, and non-convex workspace obstacle avoidance: our proposed formulation slashes Karush--Kuhn--Tucker (KKT) constraint Jacobian opcodes by up to $48.2\%$ (from $47.7\times 10^6$ to $24.7\times 10^6$), accelerates online NLP solve times by up to $1.93\times$ (and peak latency by $2.48\times$, from $22.25$~s down to $8.97$~s), and cuts offline solver build times by up to $54.7\%$. Finally, a closed-loop dual-twin tracking study demonstrates a $99.35\%$ tracking error reduction ($\max |\Delta q| = 0.0163$~rad $= 0.93^\circ$) using white-box Computed Torque Control over tuned model-free PID during high-acceleration maneuvers.
	\end{abstract}
	
	%==============================================================================
	\section*{Introduction}
	%==============================================================================
	
	For over six decades, analytical robot mechanics, nonlinear control theory, and foundational robotics pedagogy have relied on minimal kinematic parameterizations---most notably the standard Denavit--Hartenberg (DH) convention \cite{denavit1955kinematic, craig2005introduction, spong2020robot, siciliano2010robotics}---coupled with closed-form Euler--Lagrange equations of motion. In stark contrast, contemporary robotics software ecosystems, including the Robot Operating System (ROS) \cite{quigley2009ros}, MoveIt \cite{chitta2012moveit}, Gazebo \cite{koenig2004design}, PyBullet \cite{coumans2016pybullet}, MuJoCo \cite{todorov2012mujoco}, and MATLAB/Simscape Multibody \cite{simscape2023multibody}, predominantly represent articulated mechanisms using the Unified Robot Description Format (URDF) \cite{tola2024understanding, chignoli2024urdf}. This divergence has created a profound methodological divide between **black-box numerical physics simulators** and **white-box analytical mechanics** \cite{iliuta2024digital, dimosthenopoulos2024digital}.
	
	In the black-box simulation paradigm, multibody physics engines ingest raw XML-based URDF descriptions and execute forward time-stepping integration via recursive $O(n)$ spatial-vector algorithms, such as Featherstone's Articulated Body Algorithm (ABA) and Recursive Newton--Euler Algorithm (RNEA) \cite{featherstone2014rigid, carpentier2019pinocchio}, or constrained optimization solvers \cite{todorov2012mujoco}. While these numerical engines achieve exceptional forward rollout speeds and handle complex contact geometries, they function operationally as opaque numerical oracles: at any instant $t$, they accept generalized joint coordinates $\bm{q} \in \mathbb{R}^n$, velocities $\dot{\bm{q}} \in \mathbb{R}^n$, and actuator generalized forces $\bm{\tau} \in \mathbb{R}^n$, returning numerical joint accelerations $\ddot{\bm{q}} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ without exposing the underlying closed-form algebraic structure of the configuration-dependent generalized inertia matrix $\bm{M}(\bm{q}) \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$, the Christoffel-derived Coriolis and centrifugal matrix $\bm{C}(\bm{q},\dot{\bm{q}}) \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$, or the gravitational torque vector $\bm{G}(\bm{q}) \in \mathbb{R}^n$:
	\begin{equation}
		\bm{M}(\bm{q})\ddot{\bm{q}} + \bm{C}(\bm{q},\dot{\bm{q}})\dot{\bm{q}} + \bm{G}(\bm{q}) = \bm{\tau}.
		\label{eq:euler_lagrange}
	\end{equation}
	
	This lack of algebraic transparency constitutes what we term the \textit{black-box simulation paradox}. On one hand, modern engineers and students can instantiate a high-degree-of-freedom manipulator in simulation within minutes without deriving a single homogeneous transformation or Jacobian matrix. On the other hand, rigorous nonlinear control synthesis---including Computed Torque Control (exact feedback linearization) \cite{craig2005introduction, siciliano2010robotics}, Slotine--Li passivity-based adaptive control \cite{slotine1987adaptive}, observer-based disturbance rejection, physics-informed dynamic model discovery \cite{omar2024robust, omar2025rsiel}, and gradient-based Nonlinear Model Predictive Control (NMPC) \cite{andersson2019casadi}---mandates explicit access to the structural components of Eq.~\eqref{eq:euler_lagrange} alongside their exact first- and second-order state sensitivities ($\nabla_{\bm{q}}\bm{M}$, $\nabla_{\bm{q}}\bm{G}$, $\nabla_{\bm{x}}\bm{f}$, and $\nabla_{\bm{x}\bm{x}}^2\mathcal{L}$). When practitioners resort to model-free black-box control laws (such as decentralized high-gain PID) to bypass analytical modeling, uncompensated spatial inertia variations and quadratic velocity couplings ($c_{ijk}(\bm{q})\dot{q}_j\dot{q}_k$) induce severe tracking degradation, phase lag, and actuator chattering during high-acceleration multi-axis trajectories \cite{tadese2022two, sousa2019physical}.
	
	Furthermore, the emergence of industrial digital twins has underscored that nominal URDF files merely reflect idealized computer-aided design (CAD) specifications rather than the true physical dynamics of a deployed robot instance \cite{iliuta2024digital, dimosthenopoulos2024digital, asr2026calibration}. In physical hardware, manufacturing tolerances, assembly misalignments, payload variations, and structural compliance cause systematic deviations from nominal URDF parameters \cite{tadese2022two, zhou2026gtpn}. Closing this sim-to-real gap requires formulating differentiable analytical models in optimization frameworks such as CasADi \cite{andersson2019casadi} to calibrate kinematic offsets, link masses, and center-of-mass (CoM) coordinates against experimental metrology (e.g., laser-tracker static equilibrium postures and dynamic closed-loop trajectories) \cite{asr2026calibration, omar2024robust}.
	
	Despite the clear necessity of bridging CAD-derived URDF descriptions with white-box analytical formulations, automating this translation presents four formidable mathematical and computational barriers:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item \textbf{Topological Graph Complexity and Frame Convention Mismatches:} Production URDF models (as analyzed across large-scale repositories by Tola and Corke \cite{tola2024understanding}) are structured as branched directed acyclic graphs (DAGs) containing actuated joints, fixed mounting flanges, sensor brackets, and visual/collision mesh nodes. Each URDF joint defines an unconstrained 6-DOF transformation ($\bm{t}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$ and roll-pitch-yaw Euler angles $\bm{\varphi}_i \in \mathbb{R}^3$) referenced to arbitrary CAD assembly origins, with joint actuation axes $\hat{\bm{u}}_i \in S^2$ oriented in arbitrary spatial directions \cite{yu2024unified, ihab2026urdf2dt}. Conversely, the standard DH convention compresses each link transformation into four minimal parameters $(a_i, \alpha_i, d_i, \theta_i)$ subject to two strict geometric invariants: Rule R1 ($\hat{\bm{x}}_i \perp \hat{\bm{z}}_{i-1}$) and Rule R2 ($\hat{\bm{x}}_i \cap \hat{\bm{z}}_{i-1} \neq \emptyset$) \cite{denavit1955kinematic, craig2005introduction}. While early work by Corke \cite{corke2007simple} factorized general transformations into elementary sequences, and recent tools such as \texttt{urdf\_to\_dh} \cite{trzy2020urdf} and \texttt{URDFGen} \cite{cursi2021urdfgen} address partial kinematic conversions, existing open-source tools either rely on auxiliary ``dummy'' frames, fail on general branched trees, or stop strictly at kinematics without propagating inertial properties into the synthesized DH coordinate frames \cite{ihab2026urdf2dt}.
		\item \textbf{Non-Uniqueness and Continuous Gauge Freedoms in DH Synthesis:} A fundamental geometric property of the DH convention is that frame assignments are non-unique whenever consecutive joint axes $(\hat{\bm{z}}_{i-1}, \hat{\bm{z}}_i)$ are parallel or collinear \cite{ihab2026urdf2dt, yu2024unified}. For parallel axes, the common normal direction is unique, but its anchor point admits a continuous translational gauge freedom $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$ along the joint axis; for collinear axes, an additional rotational gauge freedom $\phi \in [-\pi, \pi)$ emerges about the shared axis. Consequently, authoritative references---such as Craig \cite{craig2005introduction}, Spong \cite{spong2020robot}, and manufacturer documentation---assign different frame origins and parameter tables to the exact same physical manipulator (e.g., the shoulder and elbow links of the Universal Robots UR5), yet yield identical global end-effector forward kinematics. Naive conversion scripts that lack formal regime classification and constraint-preserving gauge parametrization frequently corrupt downstream link transformations and tool frames \cite{abdelrahman2026urdf2dt}.
		\item \textbf{Inertial Frame Propagation and Physical Realizability Violations:} Because URDF link masses $m_i$, CoM vectors $\bm{c}_i^{\text{urdf}}$, and rotational inertia tensors $\bm{I}_i^{\text{urdf}}$ are defined relative to local CAD link origins rather than standard DH frames, constructing valid Euler--Lagrange equations requires exact spatial similarity transformations and moment-arm shifts relative to the preceding DH origin \cite{ihab2026urdf2dt, asr2026calibration}. Moreover, empirical URDF datasets and classical identification benchmarks (such as historical Puma 560 datasets) frequently contain corrupted or truncated inertia tensors that violate rigid-body physical realizability---specifically positive-definiteness and the principal-moment triangle inequality ($I_1 + I_2 \ge I_3$) \cite{sousa2019physical, tola2024understanding}---causing numerical breakdowns during forward dynamic integration.
		\item \textbf{Symbolic Expression Explosion and $O(n^4)$ Christoffel Bottlenecks:} When deriving closed-form Euler--Lagrange equations via classical computer algebra systems (e.g., the MATLAB Symbolic Math Toolbox or SymPy), differentiating the inertia matrix to form the $n^3$ Christoffel symbols of the first kind ($c_{ijk} = \frac{1}{2}(\frac{\partial M_{ij}}{\partial q_k} + \frac{\partial M_{ik}}{\partial q_j} - \frac{\partial M_{kj}}{\partial q_i})$) triggers severe trigonometric expression swell for $n \ge 6$ DOFs. Similarly, symbolic matrix inversion ($\bm{M}(\bm{q})^{-1}$) for forward dynamics produces massive rational expressions that degrade numerical conditioning and preclude real-time execution. While modern spatial-vector libraries such as Pinocchio \cite{carpentier2019pinocchio} and Corke's Robotics Toolbox \cite{corke1996robotics, corke2017robotics} circumvent symbolic swell via recursive numerical or spatial formulations, they do not directly provide compressed, DH-consistent closed-form Euler--Lagrange matrices across MATLAB, Simulink, and Python ecosystems.
	\end{enumerate}
	
	To resolve these four challenges within a unified scientific and pedagogical architecture, we present an autonomous, self-verifying **URDF-to-Analytical Digital Twin Framework** realized across **three complementary architectural tiers** (illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:fig1_architecture}):
	\begin{itemize}
		\item \textbf{Tier 1 --- Pure MATLAB Symbolic (\texttt{MATLAB-Sym}):} Designed for maximal glass-box algebraic inspectability, theoretical analysis, and robotics pedagogy. Implemented natively using the MATLAB Symbolic Math Toolbox, this tier outputs explicit closed-form algebraic expressions for $\bm{T}_0^i(\bm{q})$, $\bm{J}(\bm{q})$, $\bm{M}(\bm{q})$, $c_{ijk}(\bm{q})$, and $\bm{G}(\bm{q})$, alongside compiled \texttt{matlabFunction} handles.
		\item \textbf{Tier 2 --- MATLAB/CasADi + Simscape Multibody (\texttt{MATLAB-CasADi}):} Designed for accelerated industrial Model-Based Design (MBD) and hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) workflows. By formulating the DH-projected Euler--Lagrange equations within CasADi's symbolic algorithmic differentiation (\texttt{SX}) framework \cite{andersson2019casadi}, Tier 2 eliminates symbolic expression swell, compiles standalone ANSI C code, and programmatically synthesizes a 1:1 cross-validated Simscape Multibody physical twin (\texttt{dt\_build.m}) \cite{asr2026calibration}.
		\item \textbf{Tier 3 --- Python/CasADi + MuJoCo (\texttt{Python-MuJoCo}):} Designed for open-source scalability, zero-licensing deployment, and real-time closed-loop nonlinear control ($>1$~kHz). Tier 3 introduces derivation-phase symbolic graph compression (direct generalized momentum directional derivatives, symbolic Cholesky factorization, and parameterized 3D gravity $\bm{g} \in \mathbb{R}^3$) and couples CasADi's differentiable analytical engine with headless MuJoCo physics (\texttt{mj\_inverse} and \texttt{mj\_step}) \cite{todorov2012mujoco} under a cohesive **Dual-Twin Paradigm**.
	\end{itemize}
	
	\noindent\textbf{Contributions Beyond Preliminary Work:} In recent preliminary studies, the authors introduced an interactive studio for UR5 DH frame assignment \cite{ihab2026urdf2dt} and demonstrated static Levenberg--Marquardt parameter calibration on the UR5 in Simscape \cite{asr2026calibration}. The present manuscript advances substantially beyond those foundational studies by establishing:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item \textbf{A Tri-Paradigm Multi-Language Ecosystem:} A unified mathematical and software pipeline spanning Pure MATLAB Symbolic, MATLAB/CasADi Simscape, and Python/CasADi MuJoCo, systematically quantifying the engineering trade-offs between human algebraic readability, MBD integration, and real-time control throughput;
		\item \textbf{Derivation-Phase $O(n^2)$ Symbolic Graph Compression and Multi-DOF Scaling:} A rigorous mathematical reformulation of the Coriolis and centrifugal bias vector via the Euler--Lagrange generalized momentum directional derivative ($\operatorname{jtimes}(\bm{M}\dot{\bm{q}}, \bm{q}, \dot{\bm{q}}) - \frac{1}{2}\nabla_{\bm{q}}(\dot{\bm{q}}^T\bm{M}\dot{\bm{q}})$) paired with inversion-free symbolic Cholesky factorization ($\bm{M} = \bm{R}^T\bm{R}$). We provide formal proofs reducing asymptotic complexity from $O(n^4)$ to $O(n^2)$ and validate the scaling law across serial chains spanning $n \in [3, 14]$ DOFs, demonstrating an empirical power law of $\mathcal{O}(n^{2.28})$ vs.\ $\mathcal{O}(n^{3.24})$ for classical Christoffel symbols, slashing forward dynamics opcodes by up to $75.5\%$, and achieving up to $3.94\times$ execution speedups ($1.42$~ms $\to 360.7~\mu$s at $n=14$) at exact machine precision ($< 10^{-14}$~N$\cdot$m);
		\item \textbf{Autonomous Link-by-Link Sequential Dynamic Isolation:} A fail-fast 5-stage diagnostic verification protocol that sequentially freezes inactive joints ($j \neq i$) and zeroes downstream link inertias ($k > i$), eliminating cross-link dynamic masking and enabling deterministic localization of frame or inertial mapping faults;
		\item \textbf{Multi-Robot Generalization and Physical Realizability Sanitization:} Zero-hardcoding validation across four structurally diverse industrial manipulators (Universal Robots UR5, heavy-payload UR16e, spherical-wrist ABB IRB 120, and offset-link Unimation Puma 560) with automated spectral projection onto the physically realizable rigid-body inertia cone;
		\item \textbf{Extraterrestrial Gravity Generalization and Dual-Twin Nonlinear Control:} Empirical demonstration of batch forward simulation across terrestrial, lunar, Martian, and orbital microgravity regimes ($12.4\times$ to $15.7\times$ faster than real-time), alongside a closed-loop Computed Torque Control benchmark on the UR16e achieving a $99.35\%$ tracking error reduction over tuned black-box PID;
		\item \textbf{Receding-Horizon Nonlinear Model Predictive Control (NMPC) Validation:} A rigorous empirical closed-loop optimal control study on the 6-DOF UR16e and the redundant 7-DOF KUKA LBR iiwa 7 tracking multi-harmonic trajectories under non-convex Cartesian obstacle avoidance and strict actuator torque saturation. We demonstrate that our symbolic momentum and Cholesky compression slashes KKT constraint Jacobian opcodes by $40.6\%$ to $48.2\%$, reduces average online NLP solve times by $1.61\times$ to $1.93\times$ (and peak latency by up to $2.48\times$, from $22.25$~s down to $8.97$~s), and cuts offline solver build times by up to $54.7\%$.
	\end{enumerate}
	
