	Across all four platforms, the pipeline resolves distinct mechanical and geometric challenges:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item \textbf{Autonomous Resolution of Gauge Freedoms (Regime 3):} Both the UR5 and UR16e possess two pairs of consecutive parallel axes ($\hat{\bm{z}}_1 \parallel \hat{\bm{z}}_2$ and $\hat{\bm{z}}_2 \parallel \hat{\bm{z}}_3$, representing Regime 3 parallel axes with $|c| \approx 1$). While manual DH assignments suffer from an infinite continuum of origin placements along the joint axis line, our foot-point algorithm deterministically projects the preceding origin onto the succeeding axis line ($s^* = 0$, $t^* = (\bm{O}_{i-1} - \bm{O}_i)\cdot\hat{\bm{z}}_i$), yielding exact link lengths $a_2 = 0.4250$~m, $a_3 = 0.3923$~m for UR5 and $a_2 = 0.4784$~m, $a_3 = 0.3600$~m for UR16e without axial drift.
		\item \textbf{Spherical vs.\ Non-Spherical Wrist Architectures:} The ABB IRB 120 features an intersecting 3-axis spherical wrist where the axes of Joints~4, 5, and 6 intersect at a common wrist center ($a_4 = a_5 = a_6 = 0$, Regime 2). In contrast, the UR5 and UR16e incorporate orthogonal, non-spherical wrist geometries with non-zero lateral displacements ($d_4 = 0.1091$~m, $d_5 = 0.0946$~m, $a_6 = 0.0823$~m for UR5; $d_4 = 0.1741$~m, $d_5 = 0.1198$~m, $d_6 = 0.1165$~m for UR16e). The pipeline automatically handles both topologies without structural re-parameterization.
		\item \textbf{Critical Necessity of Autonomous Sign Detection:} As highlighted in Table~\ref{tab:table4_morphology}, different robot manufacturers employ conflicting native sign conventions in their CAD and URDF definitions. While UR5 and UR16e invert only Joint~6 ($\bm{s} = [+1, +1, +1, +1, +1, -1]$), the ABB IRB 120 inverts Joints~5 and 6 ($\bm{s} = [+1, +1, +1, +1, -1, -1]$), and the Puma 560 inverts four out of six joints ($\bm{s} = [+1, -1, -1, +1, -1, -1]$). Attempting manual sign assignment across diverse platforms inevitably introduces sign flips that destabilize feedback linearization; our autonomous algorithm $\operatorname{sgn}(\operatorname{round}(\hat{\bm{z}}_i^{\text{urdf}} \cdot \hat{\bm{z}}_{i-1}^{\text{DH}}))$ deterministically resolves the exact sign matrix $\bm{S} = \operatorname{diag}(s_1, \dots, s_n)$ at zero configuration in $<1$~ms.
		\item \textbf{Autonomous Physical Realizability Sanitization:} For the Unimation Puma 560, the historical CAD inertia tensor for Link~3 documented in classical robotics literature violates the rigid-body triangle inequality: $I_{xx} + I_{yy} = 0.0018 + 0.0013 = 0.0031 < I_{zz} = 0.0036$~kg$\cdot$m$^2$ (a physical impossibility representing negative mass distribution). Our pipeline automatically intercepted this violation at Stage~1 and applied spectral eigenvalue reconstruction, restoring strict physical realizability and positive-definiteness ($\lambda_{\min} = 0.038$~kg$\cdot$m$^2$) without altering principal inertia axes.
	\end{itemize}
	
	Across all testbeds, forward kinematics position errors matched MuJoCo to machine precision ($< 10^{-15}$~m for UR5, ABB IRB 120, and Puma 560; $1.86 \times 10^{-10}$~m for UR16e due to single-precision float rounding in URDF XML strings). Unit quaternion geodesic orientation errors remained strictly below $5 \times 10^{-8}$~rad across 1,000 Monte Carlo configurations. Furthermore, every synthesized mass matrix $\bm{M}(\bm{q})$ was verified to be strictly Symmetric Positive-Definite (SPD) across the full joint workspace, enabling single-step forward dynamics evaluation in $72.1$ to $100.3~\mu$s via symbolic Cholesky factorization.
	
	\subsection*{Systematic Multi-DOF Scalability and Asymptotic Complexity Benchmark}
	
	A paramount question regarding analytical robot digital twins is whether symbolic formulations scale gracefully beyond standard 6-DOF industrial manipulators to kinematically redundant arms ($n = 7$), hyper-redundant articulated mechanisms ($n = 10$), and high-dimensional multi-limb or dual-arm manipulation platforms ($n = 12$ to $14$). While Theorem~\ref{thm:complexity} proves that replacing rank-3 Christoffel symbol tensors with the Euler--Lagrange generalized momentum directional derivative reduces theoretical complexity from $O(n^4)$ to $O(n^2)$, empirical execution on modern compiler graph engines involves common subexpression elimination, register pressure, and non-trivial sparse memory graphs.
	
	To provide definitive empirical validation of asymptotic scalability, we conducted a systematic scaling benchmark across serial robotic chains spanning $n \in \{3, 4, 6, 7, 10, 12, 14\}$ degrees of freedom. For each system dimension, complete URDF models with physically realizable link parameters (tapering link masses satisfying strict rigid-body triangle inequalities) were parsed, synthesized into analytical mechanics representations, and evaluated. Table~\ref{tab:table5_scalability} presents the comprehensive quantitative breakdown of symbolic graph sizes (opcodes), derivation latencies, and execution runtimes, and Figure~\ref{fig:fig6_scalability_complexity} visualizes the scaling trends.
	
	\begin{figure*}[!t]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig6_scalability_and_complexity.pdf}
		\caption{\textbf{Systematic Multi-DOF Scalability, Instruction Compression, and Asymptotic Complexity Verification ($n \in [3, 14]$).} \textbf{(a)} Log-log power-law regression of symbolic instruction count (opcodes) versus system degrees of freedom $n$. The proposed Euler--Lagrange momentum directional derivative empirically scales as $\mathcal{O}(n^{2.28})$ compared to $\mathcal{O}(n^{3.24})$ for classical Christoffel symbols, while proposed symbolic Cholesky forward dynamics scales as $\mathcal{O}(n^{2.32})$ versus $\mathcal{O}(n^{3.21})$ for explicit matrix inversion. \textbf{(b)} Monotonic symbolic graph reduction (\%) as a function of system dimension $n$, growing from $-0.2\%$ at $n=3$ to $-76.8\%$ for Coriolis evaluation and $-75.5\%$ for forward dynamics at $n=14$. \textbf{(c)} Single-step forward dynamics execution latency ($\mu$s) on an x86-64 CPU core and corresponding Cholesky speedup factor, demonstrating up to $3.94\times$ acceleration ($1,419.7~\mu$s $\to 360.7~\mu$s) on a 14-DOF serial chain. \textbf{(d)} Offline derivation runtime ($t_{\text{deriv}}$, remaining strictly sub-second at $0.791$~s for $n=14$) and emitted ANSI C source code footprint across system dimensions.}
		\label{fig:fig6_scalability_complexity}
	\end{figure*}
	
	\begin{table*}[!t]
		\caption{\textbf{Systematic Multi-DOF Quantitative Scalability and Complexity Benchmark Across Serial Chains ($n \in [3, 14]$).}}
		\label{tab:table5_scalability}
		\centering
		\scriptsize
		\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2.5pt}
		\begin{tabular*}{\textwidth}{@{\extracolsep{\fill}}c c c c c c c c c c c c}
			\toprule
			\shortstack{\textbf{DOFs}\\\bm{$n$}} & \shortstack{\textbf{$\bm{M}(\bm{q})$}\\\textbf{Op}} & \shortstack{\textbf{$\bm{G}(\bm{q})$}\\\textbf{Op}} & \shortstack{\textbf{$\mathbf{c}_{\text{bias}}$ Base}\\\textbf{($n^{3.24}$)}} & \shortstack{\textbf{$\mathbf{c}_{\text{bias}}$ Prop}\\\textbf{($n^{2.28}$)}} & \shortstack{\textbf{C Red.}\\\textbf{(\%)}} & \shortstack{\textbf{FD Inv}\\\textbf{($n^{3.21}$)}} & \shortstack{\textbf{FD Chol}\\\textbf{($n^{2.32}$)}} & \shortstack{\textbf{FD Red.}\\\textbf{(\%)}} & \shortstack{\textbf{Deriv.}\\\textbf{(s)}} & \shortstack{\textbf{FD Chol}\\\textbf{($\mu$s)}} & \shortstack{\textbf{Speedup}\\\textbf{Factor}} \\
			\midrule
			3  & 505    & 106  & 1,962   & 1,965  & $-0.2\%$  & 2,179   & 2,109  & $-3.2\%$  & 0.016 & 56.0  & $1.19\times$ \\
			4  & 921    & 177  & 4,530   & 3,639  & $-19.7\%$ & 5,042   & 3,925  & $-22.2\%$ & 0.015 & 79.3  & $1.52\times$ \\
			6  & 2,177  & 343  & 15,631  & 8,713  & $-44.3\%$ & 17,225  & 9,520  & $-44.7\%$ & 0.036 & 136.6 & $1.17\times$ \\
			7  & 3,071  & 438  & 25,720  & 12,313 & $-52.1\%$ & 28,196  & 13,529 & $-52.0\%$ & 0.108 & 154.3 & $1.71\times$ \\
			10 & 7,135  & 771  & 86,435  & 28,607 & $-66.9\%$ & 93,377  & 31,824 & $-65.9\%$ & 0.321 & 263.7 & $2.30\times$ \\
			12 & 11,259 & 1033 & 165,060 & 45,045 & $-72.7\%$ & 176,876 & 50,391 & $-71.5\%$ & 0.657 & 325.5 & $3.01\times$ \\
			14 & 16,748 & 1327 & 288,494 & 66,852 & \textbf{-76.8\%} & 307,056 & 75,103 & \textbf{-75.5\%} & 0.791 & 360.7 & \textbf{3.94$\times$} \\
			\bottomrule
		\end{tabular*}
	\end{table*}
	
	The multi-DOF scaling study reveals four fundamental computational characteristics:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item \textbf{Empirical Asymptotic Scaling:} Regressing $\log(\text{Op}) = \alpha \log(n) + \beta$ across the measured data confirms the theoretical complexity bounds (Figure~\ref{fig:fig6_scalability_complexity}a). While the classical Christoffel Coriolis formulation scales with an empirical exponent of $\alpha = 3.24$, our proposed generalized momentum directional derivative scales with $\alpha = 2.28$, demonstrating near-quadratic $\mathcal{O}(n^2)$ behavior in practice. Similarly, for forward dynamics, explicit matrix inversion scales with $\alpha = 3.21$ ($\approx \mathcal{O}(n^3)$), whereas our symbolic Cholesky formulation scales as $\alpha = 2.32$.
		\item \textbf{Monotonically Increasing Graph Compression:} At small system dimensions ($n \le 3$), the overhead of directional derivative graph construction balances Christoffel permutations (1,962 vs.\ 1,965 opcodes). However, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:fig6_scalability_complexity}b, as system dimensionality expands, graph compression grows monotonically: the opcode reduction reaches $-44.3\%$ at $n=6$, $-52.1\%$ at $n=7$, $-66.9\%$ at $n=10$, and peaks at \textbf{$-76.8\%$ (a $4.32\times$ reduction in opcodes) at $n=14$}. Forward dynamics instruction footprint exhibits identical compression, slashing opcodes by \textbf{$-75.5\%$} (from 307,056 to 75,103 opcodes) on a 14-DOF system.
		\item \textbf{Expanding Runtime Speedups in High DOFs:} As system dimensionality increases, the computational savings translate directly into accelerated runtime throughput (Figure~\ref{fig:fig6_scalability_complexity}c). While the speedup factor is $1.19\times$ at $n=3$ and $1.17\times$ at $n=6$, it expands to $2.30\times$ at $n=10$, $3.01\times$ at $n=12$, and reaches \textbf{$3.94\times$ at $n=14$}. Crucially, on a 14-DOF manipulator, explicit matrix inversion requires $1,419.7~\mu$s (dropping throughput below $705$~Hz, violating the $1$~kHz real-time threshold), whereas our symbolic Cholesky formulation executes in just \textbf{$360.7~\mu$s ($2.77$~kHz)}, maintaining hard real-time determinism even on high-dimensional multi-arm architectures.
		\item \textbf{Sub-Second Offline Derivation Across All Dimensions:} Despite the dramatic expansion in mechanical complexity, offline model derivation remains strictly sub-second across all tested scales: $0.016$~s at $n=3$, $0.108$~s at $n=7$, and $0.791$~s at $n=14$ (Figure~\ref{fig:fig6_scalability_complexity}d). Emitted ANSI C99 code footprints scale from $46.9$~KB ($n=3$) to $1,722.7$~KB ($n=14$), operating with zero dynamic memory allocation and strictly bounded static stack footprints suitable for embedded real-time microcontrollers.
		\item \textbf{Exact Numerical Parity at Machine Precision:} Across every tested degree of freedom ($n=3$ through $n=14$), the torque residual between the classical Christoffel Coriolis evaluation and our proposed momentum directional derivative remained strictly bounded between $4.44 \times 10^{-16}$ and $4.44 \times 10^{-15}$~N$\cdot$m, confirming exact algebraic equivalence without any numerical drift or truncation error.
	\end{enumerate}
	
	\subsection*{Gravitational Invariance and Extraterrestrial Robotics Simulation}
	
	In orbital space robotics (e.g., satellite servicing on the International Space Station or Lunar Gateway) and non-standard industrial cell layouts (e.g., inverted ceiling gantries, wall-mounted welding arms, or inclined linear tracks), the gravitational acceleration vector expressed in the manipulator base frame $\{\mathcal{F}_0\}$ deviates from standard terrestrial vertical gravity $\bm{g} = [0, 0, -9.81]^T$~m/s$^2$. Because our Tier 3 engine parameterizes $\bm{g} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ symbolically within the potential energy gradient $\bm{G}(\bm{q},\bm{g}) = -\sum_{i=1}^n \bm{J}_{v,i}(\bm{q})^T m_i \bm{g}$, the compiled solver adapts without graph recompilation.
	
	Evaluating 5,000-step batch forward rollouts ($5.0$~s horizon, $1$~kHz sampling) on the UR16e across four canonical planetary regimes demonstrates:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item \textbf{Terrestrial Gravity ($\bm{g} = [0, 0, -9.81]^T$~m/s$^2$):} Complete 5,000-step rollout integrates in $0.4035$~s (\textbf{$12.4\times$ faster than real-time}).
		\item \textbf{Martian Surface Gravity ($\bm{g} = [0, 0, -3.71]^T$~m/s$^2$):} Gravitational load drops by $62.2\%$; batch integration completes in $0.3621$~s (\textbf{$13.8\times$ real-time}).
		\item \textbf{Lunar Surface Gravity ($\bm{g} = [0, 0, -1.62]^T$~m/s$^2$):} Gravitational load drops by $83.5\%$; batch integration completes in $0.3443$~s (\textbf{$14.5\times$ real-time}).
		\item \textbf{Orbital Microgravity ($\bm{g} = [0, 0, 0]^T$~m/s$^2$):} Potential torques vanish ($\bm{G}(\bm{q}) \equiv \bm{0}$) and dynamics are governed purely by kinetic metric curvature; batch integration completes in $0.3184$~s (\textbf{$15.7\times$ real-time}), narrowing the runtime ratio against native-C MuJoCo to \textbf{$3.9\times$}.
	\end{enumerate}
	
	\subsection*{Sequential Link-by-Link Dynamic Isolation and Deterministic Fault Diagnosis}
	
	In full-chain dynamic validation (as previously employed in standard UR5 benchmarks \cite{ihab2026urdf2dt, asr2026calibration}), exciting all joints simultaneously introduces severe diagnostic ambiguity: because $\bm{M}(\bm{q})$ and $\bm{C}(\bm{q},\dot{\bm{q}})$ couple all joints dynamically, an inertial mapping error or frame rotation bug on an intermediate link (e.g., Link~3) propagates into the torques of Joints~1 through 4, while subtle CoM errors in light distal wrist links (Links~4--6) are masked by the large gravitational torques ($>40$~N$\cdot$m) of proximal links.
	
	To guarantee unambiguous, link-specific parameter certification, our framework introduces an autonomous \textbf{Sequential Link-by-Link Dynamic Isolation Protocol}. For each link stage $i \in \{1, \dots, n\}$:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item \textbf{Downstream Mass/Inertia Zeroing:} All links distal to link $i$ ($k \in \{i+1, \dots, n\}$) have their masses and inertia tensors explicitly nulled ($m_k = 0$, $\bm{P}_{C_k} = \bm{0}$, $\bm{I}_k = \bm{0}_{3 \times 3}$) in both the analytical model and the digital twin;
		\item \textbf{Static Non-Zero Posture Locking:} All joints $j \neq i$ are locked at fixed holding postures ($q_j(t) = q_{j,\text{hold}}$, $\dot{q}_j(t) = \ddot{q}_j(t) = 0$) so that single-axis velocity and acceleration terms are strictly isolated to joint $i$:
		\begin{equation}
			\tau_i^{(i)}(t) = M_{ii}^{(i)}(\bm{q})\ddot{q}_i(t) + \frac{1}{2}\frac{\partial M_{ii}^{(i)}(\bm{q})}{\partial q_i}\dot{q}_i(t)^2 + G_i^{(i)}(\bm{q}),
			\label{eq:isolated_link_torque}
		\end{equation}
		where $M_{ii}^{(i)} = m_i \|\bm{J}_{v,i}(:,i)\|^2 + \bm{J}_{\omega,i}(:,i)^T \bm{I}_i^0 \bm{J}_{\omega,i}(:,i)$ depends exclusively on the inertial parameters of link $i$;
		\item \textbf{Persistent Single-Axis Excitation:} Joint $i$ is driven through a smooth 5th-order polynomial trajectory $q_i(t)$ and cross-validated against the physics engine sensor log $\tau_{i,\text{sim}}(t)$.
	\end{enumerate}
	
	\begin{figure*}[!t]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig2_sequential_isolation.pdf}
		\caption{\textbf{Sequential Link-by-Link Dynamic Isolation and Error Localization on the UR5 Manipulator.} For each stage $i=1,\dots,6$, downstream links $k > i$ are zeroed out and non-active joints $j \neq i$ are locked stationary, isolating the actuation torque $\tau_i^{(i)}(t)$ of link $i$ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:isolated_link_torque}). Across all six stages, the analytical DH Euler--Lagrange torque (blue solid curve) matches the MuJoCo inverse dynamics ground truth (red dashed curve) with maximum residuals $|\Delta \tau| \le 0.0074$~N$\cdot$m on proximal links and $\le 0.0009$~N$\cdot$m on wrist links, guaranteeing zero cross-link fault masking.}
		\label{fig:fig2_sequential_isolation}
	\end{figure*}
	
	As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:fig2_sequential_isolation}, the isolated analytical torque matches MuJoCo's inverse dynamics across all six stages of the UR5, with maximum absolute residuals bounded by $0.0074$~N$\cdot$m on Joint~1, $0.0001$~N$\cdot$m on Joints~2 and 3, and $<0.0009$~N$\cdot$m across Wrist Joints~4--6. If a synthetic fault is injected into Link~$i$ (such as omitting the similarity rotation $\bm{R}_{\text{rel}}\bm{I}_i\bm{R}_{\text{rel}}^T$ or shifting $\bm{P}_{C_i}$ by $5$~mm), Stages $1$ through $i-1$ pass with zero error while Stage~$i$ immediately triggers a fail-fast assertion, providing deterministic root-cause localization.
	
	\subsection*{White-Box Computed Torque Control vs.\ Black-Box Model-Free PID}
	
	To demonstrate how white-box analytical access translates into closed-loop control superiority under high-speed dynamic conditions, we executed a multi-axis trajectory tracking benchmark on the heavy-payload UR16e ($33.1$~kg arm mass) and standard UR5 manipulators in MuJoCo forward simulation ($dt = 1$~ms, including joint viscous damping and friction). The manipulators tracked an aggressive 5th-order polynomial and multi-sine trajectory ($T = 2.5$~s, peak joint velocity $>2.2$~rad/s, peak acceleration $>4.5$~rad/s$^2$).
	
	Three controllers were compared under identical feedback gains:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item \textbf{Controller 1 (Black-Box Model-Free PID):} Decentralized joint PID designed without access to $\bm{M}(\bm{q})$, $\bm{C}(\bm{q},\dot{\bm{q}})$, or $\bm{G}(\bm{q})$:
		\begin{equation}
			\bm{\tau}_{\text{PID}}(t) = \bm{K}_p (\bm{q}_d - \bm{q}) + \bm{K}_d (\dot{\bm{q}}_d - \dot{\bm{q}}) + \bm{K}_i \int_0^t (\bm{q}_d - \bm{q})\,d\sigma,
		\end{equation}
		with $\bm{K}_p = 400\bm{I}$, $\bm{K}_d = 45\bm{I}$, $\bm{K}_i = 150\bm{I}$ and integral anti-windup clamping.
		\item \textbf{Controller 2 (Partial White-Box --- PD + Gravity Compensation):} Uses the analytical gravity vector $\hat{\bm{G}}(\bm{q})$ from our framework (extending the control structure of Ref.~\cite{asr2026calibration}) while neglecting inertial and Coriolis coupling:
		\begin{equation}
			\bm{\tau}_{\text{PD+G}}(t) = \bm{K}_p (\bm{q}_d - \bm{q}) + \bm{K}_d (\dot{\bm{q}}_d - \dot{\bm{q}}) + \hat{\bm{G}}(\bm{q}).
		\end{equation}
		\item \textbf{Controller 3 (Full White-Box --- Computed Torque Control, CTC):} Full inverse-dynamics feedback linearization utilizing the exact compiled CasADi matrices $(\hat{\bm{M}}, \hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\text{bias}}, \hat{\bm{G}})$:
		\begin{equation}
			\bm{\tau}_{\text{CTC}}(t) = \hat{\bm{M}}(\bm{q}) \left[ \ddot{\bm{q}}_d + \bm{K}_d (\dot{\bm{q}}_d - \dot{\bm{q}}) + \bm{K}_p (\bm{q}_d - \bm{q}) \right] + \hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\text{bias}}(\bm{q},\dot{\bm{q}}) + \hat{\bm{G}}(\bm{q}).
		\end{equation}
	\end{enumerate}
	
	\begin{figure*}[!t]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig3_tracking_trajectories_ur16e.pdf}
		\caption{\textbf{High-Speed Multi-Axis Trajectory Tracking Benchmark on the Heavy-Payload UR16e Manipulator.} (a) Joint 2 (shoulder lift, heavy-inertia axis) response under aggressive 5th-order polynomial motion. Black-box PID (dotted red) and Partial White-Box PD+Gravity (dashed blue) exhibit severe dynamic phase lag and tracking droop due to unmodeled inertial and Coriolis coupling, whereas the White-Box Computed Torque Controller (solid green) tracks the reference trajectory (solid black) with imperceptible deviation. (b) Total joint position tracking error norm $\|\bm{e}(t)\|_2$ on a logarithmic scale. (c) Actuation torque profile $\tau_2(t)$ for Joint 2, highlighting smooth dynamic feedforward torque under CTC versus high-frequency oscillatory chattering under PID.}
		\label{fig:fig3_tracking_ur16e}
	\end{figure*}
	
	\begin{figure*}[!t]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig4_torque_profiles_and_error.pdf}
		\caption{\textbf{Tracking Error Reduction and Decoupled Joint Performance under Multi-Sine Excitation.} (a) Comparison of RMS tracking error across the UR16e and UR5 manipulators for Black-Box PID, Partial PD+G, and White-Box CTC, demonstrating a $>96\%$ error reduction across both platforms. (b) Per-joint tracking error reduction on the UR16e under Computed Torque Control relative to PID, consistently exceeding $96.5\%$ across all six joints.}
		\label{fig:fig4_torque_profiles_and_error}
	\end{figure*}
	
	As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:fig3_tracking_ur16e}a--b, on the heavy-payload UR16e, Black-Box PID suffers a peak tracking error of $2.5713$~rad and an RMS error of $1.7221$~rad because fixed feedback gains cannot instantaneously counteract configuration-varying link inertias and centrifugal forces. While Partial White-Box PD+G (Controller 2) performs well in quasi-static regimes \cite{asr2026calibration}, under rapid acceleration ($>4.5$~rad/s$^2$) the uncompensated $\bm{M}(\bm{q})\ddot{\bm{q}}_d + \mathbf{c}_{\text{bias}}(\bm{q},\dot{\bm{q}})$ terms act as severe disturbances. In contrast, Full White-Box CTC (Controller 3) globally linearizes and decouples the multi-body dynamics, slashing the RMS tracking error to $0.0112$~rad---an **overall tracking error reduction of $99.35\%$ on the UR16e** and **$96.15\%$ on the UR5** (Figure~\ref{fig:fig4_torque_profiles_and_error}a--b), while eliminating the destructive high-frequency torque chattering exhibited by PID (Figure~\ref{fig:fig3_tracking_ur16e}c).
	
	\subsection*{Real-Time Nonlinear Model Predictive Control Benchmark on 6-DOF and 7-DOF Manipulators}
	
	While open-loop forward dynamics and feedback-linearizing Computed Torque Control demonstrate exceptional computational throughput ($>6.7$~kHz), modern autonomous manipulation mandates optimal control under hard state/input constraints, actuator torque saturation, and dynamic obstacle avoidance. To definitively validate the practical impact of our symbolic momentum and Cholesky compression for optimal control, we formulated and executed an empirical, closed-loop receding-horizon Nonlinear Model Predictive Control (NMPC) benchmark on two structurally distinct industrial platforms:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item \textbf{Universal Robots UR16e ($n=6$, Heavy Payload):} A 6-DOF manipulator characterized by large proximal link masses ($27.26$~kg arm mass, $16.0$~kg payload capacity) subject to asymmetric joint actuator torque bounds ($\bm{\tau}_{\max} = [150, 150, 150, 28, 28, 28]^T$~N$\cdot$m);
		\item \textbf{KUKA LBR iiwa 7 ($n=7$, Kinematically Redundant):} A 7-DOF redundant lightweight collaborative robot featuring alternating skew-orthogonal joint axes ($d_1=0.34$, $d_3=0.40$, $d_5=0.40$, $d_7=0.045$~m; all $\alpha_{1..6}=-\pi/2$, $\alpha_7=0$) subject to torque limits ($\bm{\tau}_{\max} = [176, 176, 110, 110, 110, 40, 40]^T$~N$\cdot$m).
	\end{enumerate}
	
	\noindent\textbf{NMPC Problem Formulation and Discretization:} The manipulators track an aggressive multi-harmonic sinusoidal joint trajectory $\bm{q}_d(t)$ while simultaneously enforcing end-effector collision avoidance against a non-convex spherical workspace obstacle ($\|\bm{p}_{\text{ee}}(\bm{q}) - \bm{p}_{\text{obs}}\|_2 \ge r_{\text{obs}} + d_{\text{safe}}$ with obstacle radius $r_{\text{obs}} = 0.12$~m and safety margin $d_{\text{safe}} = 0.04$~m). The continuous-time optimal control problem in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:nmpc_ocp}--\eqref{eq:nmpc_bounds} is transcribed via direct multiple shooting over $N=15$ intervals with discretization step $\Delta t = 0.02$~s (prediction horizon $T_h = 0.30$~s) and 4th-order Runge--Kutta integration constraints. The state vector $\bm{x} = [\bm{q}^T, \dot{\bm{q}}^T]^T \in \mathbb{R}^{2n}$ and control vector $\bm{u} = \bm{\tau} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ yield $n_w = (N+1)(2n) + N n$ decision variables ($n_w = 282$ for UR16e, $n_w = 329$ for KUKA iiwa) and $n_g = (N+1)(2n) + N$ equality/inequality constraints ($n_g = 207$ for UR16e, $n_g = 239$ for KUKA iiwa). Cost weights are parameterized as $\bm{Q} = \operatorname{diag}(200\bm{I}_n, 10\bm{I}_n)$, $\bm{R} = 0.005\bm{I}_n$, and terminal weight $\bm{P} = 2\bm{Q}$. The resulting non-linear programs are solved online across 40 closed-loop sampling steps using IPOPT with limited-memory quasi-Newton Hessian approximation and receding-horizon warm-starting.
	
	Two dynamic formulations are benchmarked under identical trajectories, costs, and solver tolerances:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item \textbf{Baseline Formulation:} Evaluates Coriolis forces via classical Christoffel symbols ($\bm{C}(\bm{q},\dot{\bm{q}})\dot{\bm{q}}$) and resolves forward accelerations via explicit symbolic matrix inversion ($\ddot{\bm{q}} = \bm{M}(\bm{q})^{-1}(\bm{\tau} - \bm{C}\dot{\bm{q}} - \bm{G})$);
		\item \textbf{Proposed Formulation:} Evaluates Coriolis bias directly via the generalized momentum directional derivative ($\mathbf{c}_{\text{bias}}(\bm{q},\dot{\bm{q}})$ from Eq.~\eqref{eq:cbias_exact}) and resolves forward accelerations via pivot-free symbolic Cholesky factorization ($\bm{M} = \bm{R}^T\bm{R}$ from Proposition~\ref{prop:cholesky}).
	\end{itemize}
	
	\begin{figure*}[!t]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig7_nmpc_benchmark.pdf}
		\caption{\textbf{Empirical Closed-Loop Nonlinear Model Predictive Control (NMPC) Benchmark with Obstacle Avoidance across 6-DOF and 7-DOF Manipulators.} \textbf{(a)} Receding-horizon NLP solve time per sampling step $k$ on the heavy-payload UR16e ($n=6$, $N=15$ intervals, $\Delta t = 20$~ms), comparing the classical Christoffel + Inversion baseline (crimson dotted curve) against the proposed Momentum + Cholesky formulation (blue solid curve). \textbf{(b)} Per-step solve time on the kinematically redundant KUKA LBR iiwa 7 ($n=7$), demonstrating consistent per-step acceleration and mitigation of peak latency spikes. \textbf{(c)} Summary bar chart of mean NLP solve times and achievable closed-loop control frequencies across both platforms, highlighting up to $1.93\times$ average speedup and $2.48\times$ peak speedup. \textbf{(d)} Closed-loop Cartesian end-effector distance $d(t) = \|\bm{p}_{\text{ee}}(t) - \bm{p}_{\text{obs}}\|_2$ to the obstacle (green curve) on the KUKA iiwa, strictly respecting the safety boundary $r_{\text{obs}} + d_{\text{safe}} = 0.16$~m (crimson dashed boundary) with non-negative clearance margin ($+0.2578$~m minimum margin) throughout the aggressive motion.}
		\label{fig:fig7_nmpc_benchmark}
	\end{figure*}
	
	\begin{table*}[!t]
